---
import SupplierLayout from '@/layouts/SupplierLayout.astro';
import { TOWEL_SPECS } from '@/data/specs';
import { supplierTitle } from '@/lib/seo';

const title = supplierTitle('Towel');
const terms = [
  { label: 'Minimum order', value: '50 pieces per style. Mixed sizes are quoted separately and shipped together.' },
  { label: 'Sizes', value: '80 × 160 cm bath, 35 × 75 cm face, 35 × 35 cm hand and 50 × 80 cm bath mat as standard. Other sizes cut to order.' },
  { label: 'Sampling', value: 'A sample goes out before bulk. The sample cost and lead time are confirmed on the quotation.' },
  { label: 'Lead time', value: 'Quoted per order, after the sample is approved.' },
  { label: 'Payment', value: 'Terms come with the quotation and depend on order value and destination.' },
  { label: 'Documents', value: 'Commercial invoice, packing list, bill of lading. Certificates where the project needs them.' },
];
---

<SupplierLayout
  title={title}
  description="Hotel towel supplier for bath, face and hand towels and cotton bath mats, 100% cotton, 490 to 1000 GSM. Wholesale, MOQ 50 pcs."
  h1="Hotel towel supplier & wholesale manufacturer"
  breadcrumb="Hotel Towel Supplier"
  serviceName="Hotel towel supply and manufacturing"
  serviceType="Hotel towel supply and manufacturing: bath, face and hand towels and cotton bath mats"
  specs={TOWEL_SPECS}
  images={['towel1', 'hotel-towels-hero']}
  specLead="GSM is calculated from finished size and weight. Both are listed so a buyer can check the number rather than take it."
  terms={terms}
  categoryHref="/hotel-towels/"
  categoryLabel="Hotel towels range"
>
  <p>
    We make hotel towels at our plant in Jimo, Qingdao: bath, face and hand towels and the
    cotton bath mat. All four are 100% cotton. Trade accounts only, 50 pieces per style.
  </p>
  <p>
    The people who ask us for a quote are usually in one of three positions. A hotel is
    replacing a programme that has worn out. A distributor needs a price and a repeatable
    spec. A contractor is furnishing a floor and has a delivery date. The questions below are
    the ones we can answer before a sample leaves the plant.
  </p>

  <h2 class="pt-4 text-xl font-semibold text-ink-900">The range and the numbers behind it</h2>
  <p>
    The bath towel is 80 × 160 cm at 800 g, which works out to about 625 GSM. The face towel
    is 35 × 75 cm at 150 g, about 571 GSM. The hand towel is 35 × 35 cm at 60 g, about
    490 GSM. The bath mat is 50 × 80 cm at 400 g, 1000 GSM.
  </p>
  <p>
    Those four are the standard sizes. Bath sheets, smaller gym towels and wrapped amenity
    sets are cut to order. Send the finished size you want, not the nominal one. Shrinkage is
    part of the cutting plan, and a towel cut to the nominal figure can finish smaller than
    the one in the room now.
  </p>

  <h2 class="pt-4 text-xl font-semibold text-ink-900">GSM is the number to check</h2>
  <p>
    GSM is grams per square metre, so it depends on both weight and size. Take the same 800 g
    towel in two sizes: at 70 × 140 cm it is about 816 GSM, at 80 × 160 cm it is about 625.
    Same weight, different cloth.
  </p>
  <p>
    A supplier quoting an "800 g bath towel" has not told you the GSM. One quoting a GSM
    without a finished size has not told you the weight. Ask for both, then divide the weight
    by the area in square metres and check the answer yourself. It takes a calculator and ten
    seconds.
  </p>
  <p>
    Our numbers are calculated from the finished size and weight in the table below. If a
    supplier will not say how theirs were measured, assume the figure flatters the product.
  </p>

  <h2 class="pt-4 text-xl font-semibold text-ink-900">Questions worth putting to any towel supplier</h2>
  <p>
    The list below is what a towel order turns on. Ask for the answers in writing, not in a
    sales call.
  </p>
  <ul class="list-disc space-y-2 pl-5">
    <li>Finished size in centimetres and weight in grams, for every size quoted.</li>
    <li>The GSM calculated from those two numbers.</li>
    <li>Whether the GSM is measured on the greige towel, the finished towel, or after washing.</li>
    <li>Pile construction and yarn number, if the supplier will state them.</li>
    <li>Border: woven into the towel or sewn on, and the border width.</li>
    <li>Shrinkage allowance built into the cut plan.</li>
    <li>Bleach tolerance, if your laundry uses oxygen bleach.</li>
    <li>Carton count, carton size and cube per size.</li>
    <li>Care label language, fibre content, and where the property logo goes.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    A supplier who answers all of these is quoting a product. One who answers the first two
    is quoting a catalogue page.
  </p>

  <h2 class="pt-4 text-xl font-semibold text-ink-900">Where towel programmes fail</h2>
  <p>
    Hems and borders go first. A towel can still look white while the border is worn through,
    and housekeeping will pull it from the room before the body of the towel has failed. Ask
    how the border is attached, and check the seam on the sample with both hands.
  </p>
  <p>
    Par levels decide the replacement rate as much as GSM does. A property holding a thin
    stock washes the same towels harder and replaces them sooner. When you build the budget,
    count wash cycles per year and replacement rate, not just the opening order.
  </p>
  <p>
    Colour is the other quiet cost. White is standard. Any other shade is a dye run with its
    own minimum, and a shade matched to a brand standard needs a sample approved before the
    run starts.
  </p>

  <h2 class="pt-4 text-xl font-semibold text-ink-900">Borders, logos and packing</h2>
  <p>
    A dobby border is woven into the towel and stays there. A sewn-on band is a separate
    operation; look at how it is attached and at the stitch density before you approve it.
    On a white towel the border is also the only visual detail a guest sees, so a poor one
    shows up in the room.
  </p>
  <p>
    Logos can be woven labels or embroidery. Embroidery sits down in the pile and reads less
    crisply than it does on a sheet, which is why most hotel programmes use a woven end label.
    Send the artwork, the placement and the colour count, and we will say what holds at the
    size you specify.
  </p>
  <p>
    Packing changes freight. Individual polybags, pairs, or bulk in the carton all fit in a
    container differently, and carton cube belongs in the quote. We do not do retail
    packaging.
  </p>

  <h2 class="pt-4 text-xl font-semibold text-ink-900">What we need to quote</h2>
  <ul class="list-disc space-y-2 pl-5">
    <li>Sizes and quantity per size, not just a total.</li>
    <li>GSM, or the towel you are replacing, so we can match by feel and by number.</li>
    <li>Colour, and logo artwork if the programme carries one.</li>
    <li>Packing preference.</li>
    <li>Destination port and the month the first container has to arrive.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    We come back with a price per size, the weight and GSM behind it, and a sample before
    bulk. Minimum 50 pieces per style, FOB Qingdao or CIF to your port.
  </p>

  <h2 class="pt-4 text-xl font-semibold text-ink-900">Towels in the rest of the order</h2>
  <p>
    Towels usually ship with bed linen, and the bath mat with either. Putting the whole
    guest-room textile order on one inquiry keeps it to one contract, one inspection and one
    set of export documents. Bed linen construction is on the
    <a class="font-semibold underline" href="/hotel-bedding-supplier/">hotel bedding supplier</a>
    page and the sheet range on
    <a class="font-semibold underline" href="/hotel-linens/">hotel linens</a>. Anything else
    goes through
    <a class="font-semibold underline" href="/request-a-quote/">request a quote</a>.
  </p>
</SupplierLayout>
